        "description": "<img src='https://i.cbc.ca/ais/1.7641702,1758664884000/full/max/0/default.jpg?im=Crop%2Crect%3D%280%2C0%2C3961%2C2228%29%3B' alt='Chris Avery is CEO of the Arctic Gateway Group, a partnership of 29 First Nations and 12 northern Manitoba and Nunavut communities. They own the Port of Churchill and Hudson Bay Railway.' width='620' height='349' title='Chris Avery is CEO of the Arctic Gateway Group, a partnership of 29 First Nations and 12 northern Manitoba and Nunavut communities. They own the Port of Churchill and Hudson Bay Railway. They were already planning to expand, but U.S. President Donald Trump&apos;s trade war makes the timing even more urgent.'/><p>As Premier Wab Kinew pitches investors on a Port of Churchill expansion that could cost as much as $80 billion, the company that owns the port says it only needs a small fraction of that cash \u2014 less than $3 billion \u2014 to improve the port and the railway that connects it to the rest of Canada.</p>",
