        "description": "<img src='https://i.cbc.ca/ais/3356fefb-4fd9-4169-a557-d310f9d187df,1789413089497/full/max/0/default.jpg?im=Crop%2Crect%3D%280%2C29%2C4032%2C2268%29%3B' alt='A man leans against a tree.' width='620' height='349' title='St. Catharines, Ont., resident Saleh Waziruddin, a member of the Niagara Region Anti-Racism Association executive committee, says it&apos;s important to know municipal council and school board candidates&apos; views on race-related issues before the Oct. 26 election.'/><p>Ahead of next month's Ontario municipal election, Niagara candidates are being surveyed for their views on topics that are priorities for the Niagara Region Anti-Racism Association. \"We don't want to find out afterwards, after electing someone, that they have a position that a lot of people would disagree on with racism,\" says a member of the association's executive committee.</p>",
